{
  "name": "Chart marks",
  "description": "The two marks a chart draws over its plot rather than on a reading, and the scale one chart borrows from another. The inside layer sets a threshold of 25 above its own readings, so an auto scale grows to 18...25 and the line lands on the top edge; that grown range is what pins the growth. The outside layer draws its own readings against the inside layer's range through `scaleFrom`, which is how two series share one plot, and its own threshold of 20 is placed on the borrowed range rather than on the 3...14 it would have had alone. Its `nowIndex` of 9 is clamped to the last of six readings. The fixed layer's threshold of 50 falls outside the range it asked for, so no line is drawn and `thresholdY` is absent. The last four layers are the ways a link can be wrong: one points at a chart the document does not have, two point at each other, and one points at itself. All four fall back to a chart's own scale, and the cycle pair settles in document order, so the first of the two ends up on the second's range and the second on its own.",
